    <!-- ══ The logo triad ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Firedown's identity is THREE hues, and they are not a choice made
         here — they are the six colours in ic_launcher_foreground.xml:
         #FF716C / #FF525B coral, #FFB58A / #FFA386 peach, #E83A87 / #B4225E
         magenta. One warm arm (+27 deg to peach) and one cool arm (-31 deg to
         magenta) either side of the brand coral. The home shelf chips
         (home_chip_downloads / _vault / _trackers) have always mirrored it.

         Each hue has a JOB, and that is what makes three hues a system rather
         than more colours:
           primary  CORAL   — acts. The thing you press: FAB, filled buttons,
                              Continue, progress, the checked filter chip.
                              Never a passive container.
           secondary PEACH  — supports. Tonal ground behind content: the
                              recovery-code box, the home backup pill, banners.
                              Never fills a button.
           tertiary MAGENTA — identifies. "A different kind of thing": vault /
                              private, cloud + sync state. Never competes with
                              the CTA.

         History worth not repeating: secondaryContainer was briefly rotated
         onto the brand hue (h 56 -> h 29) on the grounds that the peach was
         "a hue that appears nowhere else". It appears in the app icon. The
         rotation collapsed the triad's warm arm into the primary, and every
         hierarchy problem that followed — a checked segment dE 8.2 from the
         Continue button, a checked chip out-shouting the artwork, a buy screen
         reading as three identical coral blocks — came from having only
         lightness left as an axis. Don't flatten it again. -->
    <color name="md_theme_primary">#ff716c</color>
    <color name="md_theme_onPrimary">#460005</color>
